VTID-03820 — commands.log
==========================

## Research (background agent) before writing any code

Traced the real execution engine rather than assuming its shape:
- services/gateway/src/services/dev-autopilot-execute.ts:1340
  runExecutionSession(s, executionId) — the function that actually writes
  files via GitHub Contents API and opens a PR. Takes only executionId,
  loads everything else (finding/plan/branch) from dev_autopilot_executions.
  LLM invocation is dual-path: worker-queue (isWorkerQueueEnabled() ?
  runWorkerTask(...) — a hardcoded Claude-subscription model, unrelated to
  llm_routing_policy) : callMessagesApi(prompt, vtid) (direct path, goes
  through callViaRouter('worker', ...)). No override parameter existed on
  either path.
- services/gateway/src/services/self-healing-injector-service.ts:215
  bridgeToAutopilotExecution() — the reusable pattern: insert an
  autopilot_recommendations row + a dev_autopilot_plan_versions row, then
  call bridgeActivationToExecution(findingId, null), which calls
  approveAutoExecute() (runs the FULL existing safety gate) to create the
  execution row, then patches execute_after=now.
- services/gateway/src/services/llm-router.ts:973 callViaRouter() — always
  DB-policy-driven via loadPolicy(), no per-call override existed.
- No existing "trigger Dev Autopilot execution for this specific VTID"
  route or tool was found anywhere in the repo (confirmed by reading the
  two existing Command Hub "Activate" buttons — both feed different,
  unrelated planes).
- dev_autopilot_executions has a metadata jsonb column and no
  model/provider column — confirmed via the live migration file, matching
  VTID-03819's own "use metadata, don't add a column" precedent.
- Confirmed: the Dev Autopilot pipeline itself has NO spec_status check —
  that gate lives on the unrelated generic VTID-lifecycle-start route. Any
  new operator-triggered on-ramp needs to add its own.

## Design decisions (why this shape, not a bigger/smaller one)

1. Override lives in llm-router.ts as opts.providerOverride/modelOverride,
   replacing PRIMARY only (the stage's own policy fallback still applies on
   failure) — additive to LLMRouterOpts, every existing caller omits both
   fields and is provably unaffected (see outputs/jest-vtid-03820-filter.txt,
   "existing callers unaffected" test).
2. The override is threaded through dev_autopilot_executions.metadata
   (read by the new extractLlmOnRampOverride(), dev-autopilot-execute.ts),
   NOT a new runExecutionSession() parameter — this keeps the function's
   signature untouched and means the override is fully determined by data
   already loaded from the DB for that execution row.
3. The worker-queue path is SKIPPED when an override is present
   (isWorkerQueueEnabled() && !onRampOverride) — the worker queue dispatches
   to a hardcoded Claude-subscription model that has nothing to do with
   llm_routing_policy, so an override could never take effect through it.
   This is the one behavior change to an existing branch, and it only
   fires when metadata.llm_on_ramp_override is set — never for a
   self-healing execution, which never sets it.
4. New services/gateway/src/services/operator-execution-onramp.ts reuses
   approveAutoExecute() (the FULL existing safety gate: kill_switch,
   allow/deny scope, budget, concurrency, tests_missing, PR-flood guard)
   rather than reimplementing any of it. Only two governance gates are new,
   because the reused machinery has neither:
     - OPERATOR_EXECUTION_ONRAMP_ENABLED kill switch, default OFF (unset
       or anything but the literal string 'true' stays disabled).
     - target VTID must have spec_status='approved' and not be terminal.
5. Plan content (plan_markdown + files_referenced) is NOT auto-derived —
   the caller (operator or the model composing the tool call) supplies it,
   same as a human filling in a plan before clicking Activate. Building
   the self-healing path's diagnosis-driven auto-derivation
   (deriveTestPathsForPlan et al.) for an operator-triggered generic VTID
   is out of scope here; the EXISTING tests_missing safety-gate rule still
   runs unchanged on whatever the caller supplies, so a bad file list is
   still rejected, not silently accepted.
6. New source_type 'operator_onramp' registered in
   autopilot-executable-source-types.ts, per that file's own documented
   process ("adding a new scanner... means adding its source_type here in
   a code-reviewed PR").
7. New operator-chat tool `autopilot_execute_task` (gemini-operator.ts +
   tool-registry.ts) is a thin, governance-logged wrapper around
   triggerOperatorExecution() — mirrors executeCreateTask()'s existing
   shape. Since the underlying capability is default-OFF via the kill
   switch, registering the tool is safe to ship without it being able to
   fire in production today.

## Code changes

- llm-router.ts: LLMRouterOpts gains providerOverride?/modelOverride?;
  callViaRouter() builds stageConfig from the override (primary only) when
  both are set, else uses the policy unchanged.
- dev-autopilot-execute.ts: ExecutionRow gains metadata?; new EXPORTED
  extractLlmOnRampOverride() (pure, permissive about malformed jsonb);
  callMessagesApi() gains an optional 4th-arg-shaped override param, passed
  through to callViaRouter(); the runExecutionSession() call site computes
  onRampOverride from exec.metadata and both branches route through it.
  supa() exported (was module-private) so operator-execution-onramp.ts can
  reuse the same REST helper instead of duplicating it.
- New operator-execution-onramp.ts: triggerOperatorExecution() — the two
  new governance gates, then insert recommendation+plan rows, call
  approveAutoExecute(), one combined PATCH stamping the DeepSeek override +
  execute_after=now, one OASIS event.
- autopilot-executable-source-types.ts: added 'operator_onramp'.
- gemini-operator.ts / tool-registry.ts: new autopilot_execute_task tool
  definition + executor + dispatch case.

